<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mothax</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as mothakes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The mothakes (singular mothax, a Doric Greek word meaning &quot;stepbrother&quot;) were a sociopolitical class in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, prominent particularly during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> (431–404 BCE). They were mostly the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiate</a> fathers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helot</a> mothers, or the children of impoverished Spartiates. Free but not citizens, they were brought up alongside Spartan boys as their foster brothers, usually at the expense of wealthy families; since they could not contribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syssitia">syssitia</a>, the citizens' common messes, they could not hold equal status, but they were allowed to fight alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">perioeci</a>. Some rose to prominence, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gylippus">Gylippus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callicratidas">Callicratidas</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>mothakes</b>, “foster brothers”, were boys of free birth but not of full Spartiate standing who were brought up in the agoge beside Spartiate boys, their costs usually paid by wealthy families.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li></ul></section>
